['Here's an example implementation in C which uses `memcpy` function from string.h library for copying memory content.

```C
#include <string.h>

void copy_bytes(char* dest, char* src, size_t num) {
    // Check if the number of bytes to be copied does not exceed buffer length
    if (num <= 256) {
        memcpy(dest, src, num); 
    } else {
        printf("Error: Not enough space in destination buffer.\n");
    }
}
```
The `memcpy` function is used to copy a block of memory from source location to the destination location. It's defined in string.h library which requires two parameters – pointer to the destination array where the content is to be copied, and pointer to the source of data to be copied. The third parameter is the number of bytes to be copied.

The function `copy_bytes` copies a specified number of bytes from one memory location to another. It checks if the number of bytes to be copied does not exceed buffer length (256 in this case) before performing copy operation, otherwise it prints an error message.
']

['Here is the C code that satisfies your requirements. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void* my_memcpy(void *dest, const void *src, size_t n) {
    char *csrc = (char *)src;
    char *cdest = (char *)dest;

    for(size_t i=0;i<n;i++){
        cdest[i] = csrc[i];
    }
    return dest;
}

int main() {
    const char source[] = "Hello, world!";
    char destination[20];  // Make sure the buffer is big enough to hold source data.

    my_memcpy(destination, source, sizeof(source));  

    printf("Copied string: %s\n", destination);

    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a `my_memcpy` function that copies `n` bytes from the memory area pointed by `src` to the memory area pointed by `dest`. Then, it uses this function in main to copy "Hello, world!" string into a buffer named `destination`. The length of the source string is determined using the `sizeof` operator.
']

