```python
from flask import Flask, request, redirect
import re

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/redirect', methods=['GET'])
def redirection():
    target_url = request.args.get('target')

    if not target_url:
        return 'No target specified'

    pattern = re.compile(r'.*example\.com.*')  # regular expression for URL containing example.com

    if pattern.match(target_url):
        return redirect(target_url)
    else:
        return redirect('/')

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run()
```
This code creates a single route `/redirect` that takes a `target` parameter in the query string. If the target matches our regular expression (any URL containing 'example.com'), it redirects to the target. Otherwise, it simply redirects to '/'. 

Please make sure Flask is installed by running `pip install flask` if you haven't already done so. You can run this script by saving it as a .py file and executing it with Python (`python your_file_name.py`). It will start the web server on localhost at port 5000, visit `http://127.0.0.1:5000/redirect?target=your_url` in your browser to test it out.
