['In order to read a file in C, we'll use the `fopen()` function to open the file and then `fread()` or `fscanf()` to read data from it. Here is an example of how you might accomplish this:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *file;
    char filename[100], c;

    printf("Enter the name of file you wish to access: ");
    scanf("%s", filename);

    // Attempt to open the file in read mode
    file = fopen(filename, "r"); 

    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Cannot open file \n");
        return 0;
    }

    c = fgetc(file);
    while (c != EOF) {
        printf("%c", c);
        c = fgetc(file);
    }

    fclose(file);

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that this code assumes you are entering the full path to the file, which is a security risk if you are reading from an unsafe directory. You should always sanitize and validate user input when dealing with files. In real world applications, usually users don't have access to directories like `/safe/`.
