    }

    // Trim trailing whitespace in the string.
    for (int i = strlen(argv[1]) - 1; argv[1][i] == ' '; i--) {
        argv[1][i] = 0;
    }

    // Trim newlines.
    int len = strlen(argv[1]);
    for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {
        if (argv[1][i] == '\n') argv[1][i] = 0;
    }

    // Null terminate the string.
    argv[1][strlen(argv[1]) - 1] = '\0';

    printf("Trimmed String: %s\n", argv[1]);
    return 0;
}
```
This code first checks if exactly one argument is provided. If not, it will print a usage message and terminate with an error status. Then it verifies that the argument contains only alphabetical characters to determine if it's a string or not. The string is then trimmed of any trailing whitespace by replacing them with null characters, and newlines are removed in the same manner. Finally, it adds a terminating null character to mark the end of the string.
